<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet>
  <title>24 &#160; Quadratic Forms  (LN 2.2, 2.3) </title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <page>
    <p> <m> \textbf{Poll question} </m>  
    </p>

    <p> True or False: <m>Q(x, y) = 8x^2 + 2y^2 - 8xy \gt 0 </m> for all values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. 
    </p>
    
    <p> <em> Hint: plot it in Desmos 3D! And: how does this exercise relate to symmetric matrices? </em> </p>
  </page>

  <page>
    <p> <m>{\large \textbf{24.1 &#160; Expressing Quadratic Forms in Terms of Symmetric Matrices}} </m> </p>
    <definition>
    <statement>
    <p> A <m> \textbf{quadratic form} </m> is a function <m> Q \;:\; \mathbb R ^{n} \to \mathbb R</m>, given by </p>
    <me>
          Q(\vec{x}) = \vec{x}^T A \vec{x} =
          \begin{pmatrix}
            x_1 \amp x_2 \amp \ldots \amp x_n
          \end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
            a_{11} \amp a_{12} \amp \ldots \amp a_{1n} \\
            a_{12} \amp a_{22} \amp \ldots \amp a_{2n} \\
            \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
            a_{1n} \amp a_{2n} \amp \ldots \amp a_{nn}
          \end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix}
            x_1 \\
            x_2 \\
            \vdots \\
            x_n
          \end{pmatrix}
        </me>
    <p> Matrix <m>A</m> is <m> n \times n </m> and symmetric, and <m> \vec{x} </m> is a variable vector in <m> \mathbb R^n</m>. </p> 
      </statement>
      </definition>
    <p> Example: express <m> Q = 3x_1^2 + x_2^2 + 8x_3^3 + 4x_1x_2 - 6x_2x_3 </m> in the form <m> Q = x^T A x </m>.
      </p> 
  </page>

  <page>
    <p> <m> {\large \textbf{24.2  The Principal Axes Theorem}} </m> </p>
    
    <theorem>
     <p> If <m>A</m> is a symmetric matrix then there exists an orthogonal change of
        variable <m>\vec x = P \vec y</m> that transforms <m>\vec {x}^{\,T}A \vec {x}</m> to
        <m>\vec {y}^{\,T}D \vec y</m> with no cross-product terms. </p> </theorem>

    <p><em> Proof in the lecture videos/textbook. </em> </p>

    <p> <m> {\textbf{24.2.1   Example}} </m> </p>

    <p> Consider: <m>Q = x^{2} + y^{2} + 2kxy</m> where <m>k \; {\in \mathbb { R}} </m>. </p>
    <blockquote>
    <p> a) Make a change of variable that transforms the quadratic form into another
                quadratic form that has no cross-product terms.</p>
    <p> b) For what values of <m>k</m> will <m>Q</m> satisfy <m>Q \ge 0</m> for any
                <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>?</p> </blockquote>

    <p> <em> Tip: it may help to plot the surfaces in Desmos 3D to see what the surface looks
        like as you change the value of <m>k</m>. </em> </p>
    </page>

    <page>
      <p> <m> \large \textbf{24.3 \; \; Classifying Quadratic Surfaces} </m> </p>
      
      <p> Our goal: <m> \textbf{solve optimization problems with quadratic functions}</m>. </p>

      <p> Take for example the surface: 
        <me>
            Q = x^2 + y^2 = \vec {x}\, ^T \begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{pmatrix}\vec {x}
        </me> </p>
        <!-- pmatrix is parenthesis matrix -->
      <p> If we set <m>Q</m> to be constant, <m>Q = k^{2}</m>, we obtain the equation of a circle whose radius is <m>k^{2}</m>.
        <me>
          k^2 = x^2 + y^2
        </me> </p>

        <image label="image_24_1" width="50%">
          <latex-image>
          
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
        \begin{axis}[
            axis line style = very thick, smooth, 
            xmin=-4,ymin=-4,zmin=0,xmax=4,ymax=4,zmax=10,
            axis lines=center,
            xticklabels={,,}, yticklabels={,,},zticklabels={,,},
            xtick=\empty, ytick=\empty, ztick=\empty, 
            % xlabel = $x$,ylabel=$y$,zlabel=$z$
            ]
        \addplot3 [surf,shader=flat,draw=black,draw=none,restrict z to domain=0:9, data cs=polar, domain=0:360, y domain=0:3] (x, y, y^2);
    \end{axis}    
    \end{tikzpicture}  
          </latex-image>
        </image>

      <p> The surface above has a unique minimum at the origin. What about other surfaces? </p>

      <definition> 
      <statement>
      <p> A quadratic form <m> Q </m> is 
      <ul>
        <li> <m> \textbf{positive definite} </m> if <m> Q(\vec{x}) \gt 0 </m> for all <m> \vec{x} \ne \vec{0} </m>. </li>
        <li> <m> \textbf{negative definite} </m> if <m> Q(\vec{x}) \lt 0 </m> for all <m> \vec{x} \ne \vec{0} </m>. </li>
        <li> <m> \textbf{positive semidefinite} </m> if <m> Q(\vec{x}) \ge 0 </m> for all <m> \vec{x}</m>. </li>
        <li> <m> \textbf{negative semidefinite} </m> if <m> Q(\vec{x}) \le 0 </m> for all <m> \vec{x}</m>. </li>
        <li> <m> \textbf{indefinite} </m> if <m> Q </m> takes on positive and negative values for <m> \vec{x} \ne \vec{0}</m>. </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
      </statement>
      </definition>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p> If <m>A</m> is a symmetric matrix with eigenvalues <m>\lambda_{i}</m>, then <m>Q = \vec x^{\,T}A \vec x</m> is
      <ul>
        <li> <m> \textbf{positive definite} </m> when all eigenvalues are positive </li>
        <li> <m> \textbf{negative definite} </m> when all eigenvalues are negative </li>
        <li> <m> \textbf{indefinite} </m>  when at least one eigenvalue is negative and at least one eigenvalue is positive </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
    </page>
    <page>
      <p> <m> \textbf{24.3.1 \; Example} </m> </p>
      <p> Assume <m> \vec{x} \in \mathbb {R^3} </m>. Construct the matrix of the quadratic form, and classify the quadratic form.</p>
      <me>Q = 4x_3^2 - 2x_1x_2</me>

    </page>


</worksheet>
